cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this repository or its results, please cite the paper below."
title: "Reconstruction-independent moment tests for quantum-computed dynamical spectra"
authors:
  - family-names: "Bonilla Vargas"
    given-names: "Nicolás"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0006-6155-4391"
    affiliation: "Universidad Nacional de Colombia; SRH University Heidelberg/Munich; Daita AI"
    email: "ngbonillav@unal.edu.co"
abstract: >-
  Quantum processors compute dynamical response functions by sampling subspaces that classical
  resources diagonalize and reconstruct into a spectral function A(w) with no independent cross-check.
  This work develops and calibrates a transportable, reconstruction-independent consistency test for
  such functions: it compares A(w) against its own spectral moments m_k = int w^k A(w) dw, which for a
  zero-temperature Lehmann spectrum are static ground-state expectations (m0 = <J^2>,
  m1 = (1/2)<[J,[H,J]]>), each estimated from the same computational-basis samples that produced the
  spectrum and never read back off A(w), so it can disagree with the reconstruction and refute it. One
  moment is evaluated on real IBM Heron hardware (the diagonal coverage channel); the discriminating
  off-diagonal moments are evaluated in simulation and shown presently infeasible on hardware. A
  blinded, pre-registered test calibrates the false-positive and detection rates, the necessary
  condition composes with the sufficient-condition moment certificates of Wang et al. and Mortimer et
  al., and a closed-form Christoffel miss-distance bound quantifies the residual. No quantum learning
  advantage is claimed.
version: "1.0.0"
date-released: "2026-08-22"
